   "line_id": "S01",
   "title": "WHAT IS INSIDE",
   "headline": "A HIDDEN RESISTOR",
   "narration_text": "Here is what is inside. A coin cell is a three volt source with a small resistor hidden in it. Energizer says a fresh one is near ten ohms. When the device asks for a burst of current, some voltage is lost pushing it through that resistor. The bigger the burst, the more you lose.",
   "word_count": 55,
   "visual_intent": "The chart dims. A cell drawn as a 3 V source with a small resistor inside; label 'about 10 ohm, fresh (Energizer handbook p.4)'. A burst arrow grows and the terminal voltage drops with it, by the same amount as the arrow.",
   "start_s": 7.213,
   "end_s": 23.173,
